<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Badrinath</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Badrinath is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage town in Chamoli district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, high in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaknanda">Alaknanda</a> river. Its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> as Badrinarayan is one of the four Char Dham sites and is traditionally linked with Adi Shankara. The temple is open only from spring to autumn because of heavy snow.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/badrinath.jpg" alt="Badrinath" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arpita Tripathy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badrinath_Temple-_Uttarakhand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
